Closures are indeed a special feature of JavaScript that allows functions to access variables from their own execution context and also from parent scopes. This behavior can be surprising at first because it seems like accessing variables from an outer scope should always work.
The reason closures behave this way is due to how JavaScript handles function creation and execution. When you define a function using the `function` keyword or with the arrow syntax (`=>`), you're creating a new "execution context" for that function. This means that each time the function is called, a new copy of the function's code is executed within its own isolated environment.
This isolation is what allows closures to access variables from their own execution context and also from parent scopes. The inner function has access to all the variables declared in its own execution context (including any variables defined in the global scope) as well as any variables declared in the outer function's execution context.
For example:
```javascript
let x = 10;

function outer() {
    let y = 20;

    function inner() {
        console.log(x); // prints 10
        console.log(y); // prints 20
    }

    return inner;
}

const myFunction = outer();
myFunction(); // logs 1
